   "source": [
    "# 🐍 Python Sets — Pair Programming Session\n",
    "**Activities + Quiz** · Work through each section together. Driver types, navigator thinks out loud.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "## Quick Reference\n",
    "\n",
    "| Property | Sets |\n",
    "|---|---|\n",
    "| Ordered? | ❌ No |\n",
    "| Allows duplicates? | ❌ No |\n",
    "| Mutable? | ✅ Yes (frozenset is not) |\n",
    "| Indexable? | ❌ No — no `s[0]` |\n",
    "| Literal syntax | `{1, 2, 3}` |\n",
    "| Empty set | `set()` — NOT `{}` (that's a dict!) |\n",
    "\n",
    "---"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "creating-sets-header",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Creating Sets — Warm-up\n",
    "Run these cells and observe the output before moving to the activities."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "creating-sets",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Literal syntax\n",
    "fruits = {\"apple\", \"banana\", \"cherry\", \"kiwi\"}\n",
    "print(fruits)\n",
    "\n",
    "# From a list — duplicates are silently dropped\n",
    "nums = set([1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3])\n",
    "print(nums)   # {1, 2, 3}\n",
    "\n",
    "# From a string — each character becomes an element\n",
    "chars = set(\"hello\")\n",
    "print(chars)  # {'h', 'e', 'l', 'o'} — note: only one 'l'\n",
    "\n",
    "# Empty set — must use set(), NOT {}\n",
    "empty = set()\n",
    "print(type(empty))   # <class 'set'>\n",
    "print(type({}))      # <class 'dict'>  ← the gotcha!"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "math-ops-header",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "---\n",
    "## Set Operations — Mathematical Techniques\n",
    "\n",
    "Python sets directly implement classical set theory. Each operation has **two equivalent forms**: a method and an operator symbol.\n",
    "\n",
    "| Operation | Method | Operator | Returns |\n",
    "|---|---|---|---|\n",
    "| Union | `a.union(b)` | `a \\| b` | All elements from both sets |\n",
    "| Intersection | `a.intersection(b)` | `a & b` | Only elements in both |\n",
    "| Difference | `a.difference(b)` | `a - b` | In a, but not in b |\n",
    "| Symmetric Difference | `a.symmetric_difference(b)` | `a ^ b` | In either, but not both |\n",
    "| Subset | `a.issubset(b)` | `a <= b` | True if all of a is in b |\n",
    "| Superset | `a.issuperset(b)` | `a >= b` | True if a contains all of b |\n",
    "| Disjoint | `a.isdisjoint(b)` | — | True if they share no elements |\n",
    "\n",
    "> **Tip:** Operators like `|` and `&` only work between two sets. Methods like `.union()` accept any iterable."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "math-ops-demo",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "a = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5}\n",
    "b = {4, 5, 6, 7, 8}\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"Union:                \", a | b)          # everything {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8}\n",
    "print(\"Intersection:         \", a & b)          # shared {4, 5}\n",
    "print(\"Difference (a - b):   \", a - b)          # in a only {1, 2, 3}\n",
    "print(\"Difference (b - a):   \", b - a)          # in b only {6, 7, 8}\n",
    "print(\"Symmetric difference: \", a ^ b)          # in one but not both {1, 2, 3, 6, 7, 8}\n",
    "print(\"Is a subset of b?     \", {4, 5} <= b)    # True \n",
    "print(\"Are they disjoint?    \", a.isdisjoint(b))# False (they share 4 and 5)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "inplace-ops-header",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### In-place update operators\n",
    "These modify the set directly instead of returning a new one."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "inplace-ops",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "s = {1, 2, 3}\n",
    "\n",
    "s |= {3, 4, 5}   # union update  (same as s.update({3,4,5}))\n",
    "print(s)          # {1, 2, 3, 4, 5}\n",
    "\n",
    "s &= {2, 3, 4}   # intersection update\n",
    "print(s)          # {2, 3, 4}\n",
    "\n",
    "s -= {3}         # difference update\n",
    "print(s)          # {2, 4}\n",
    "\n",
    "s ^= {2, 5}      # symmetric difference update\n",
    "print(s)          # {4, 5}  (2 was in both so removed, 5 was new so added)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "mutation-methods-header",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "---\n",
    "## Mutation Methods — Quick Reference\n",
    "\n",
    "| Method | Behaviour | Raises on missing? |\n",
    "|---|---|---|\n",
    "| `s.add(x)` | Add x | No (no-op if already there) |\n",
    "| `s.remove(x)` | Remove x | ✅ KeyError |\n",
    "| `s.discard(x)` | Remove x if present | ❌ Silent no-op |\n",
    "| `s.pop()` | Remove & return an arbitrary element | ✅ KeyError if empty |\n",
    "| `s.clear()` | Remove all elements | No |\n",
    "| `s.copy()` | Shallow copy | No |"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "act1-header",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "---\n",
    "## Activity 1 — Guest List Clash\n",
    "Two teams are building invite lists for a company event. Use set operations to answer each question. **No loops.**"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "act1-setup",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "team_alpha = {\"Alice\", \"Bob\", \"Carol\", \"Dave\", \"Eve\"}\n",
    "team_beta  = {\"Dave\", \"Eve\", \"Frank\", \"Grace\", \"Heidi\"}"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "act1-1",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Q1: Who is invited by EITHER team? (full guest list, no duplicates)\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "act1-2",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Q2: Who was invited by BOTH teams? (they'll get two emails — fix this!)\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "act1-3",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Q3: Who is ONLY in team_alpha's list (not beta's)?\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "act1-4",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Q4: Who appears on exactly ONE list? (invited once, not twice)\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "act1-5",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Q5: A VIP list arrives. Are all VIPs already covered by team_alpha?\n",
    "vip = {\"Alice\", \"Carol\"}\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "act1-answers",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "<details>\n",
    "<summary>💡 Show answers</summary>\n",
    "\n",
    "```python\n",
    "# Q1\n",
    "team_alpha | team_beta\n",
    "# {'Alice', 'Bob', 'Carol', 'Dave', 'Eve', 'Frank', 'Grace', 'Heidi'}\n",
    "\n",
    "# Q2\n",
    "team_alpha & team_beta\n",
    "# {'Dave', 'Eve'}\n",
    "\n",
    "# Q3\n",
    "team_alpha - team_beta\n",
    "# {'Alice', 'Bob', 'Carol'}\n",
    "\n",
    "# Q4\n",
    "team_alpha ^ team_beta\n",
    "# {'Alice', 'Bob', 'Carol', 'Frank', 'Grace', 'Heidi'}\n",
    "\n",
    "# Q5\n",
    "vip <= team_alpha   # or vip.issubset(team_alpha)\n",
    "# True\n",
    "```\n",
    "</details>\n",
    "\n",
    "---"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "act2-header",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Activity 2 — Deduplication & Membership\n",
    "Sets are extremely fast at membership tests (`in`) — O(1) vs O(n) for lists. This activity explores that, plus deduplication."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "act2-1",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Step 1: Remove duplicates from this list, preserving only unique values.\n",
    "# Do it in one line using set().\n",
    "\n",
    "raw_tags = [\"python\", \"data\", \"python\", \"ml\", \"data\", \"python\", \"ai\", \"ml\"]\n",
    "unique_tags = ___\n",
    "print(unique_tags)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "act2-2",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Step 2: You have a set of processed order IDs and a new batch to check.\n",
    "# Find which orders in the new batch have NOT been processed yet.\n",
    "\n",
    "processed = {1001, 1002, 1003, 1004, 1005}\n",
    "new_batch  = {1003, 1004, 1006, 1007, 1008}\n",
    "\n",
    "unprocessed = ___\n",
    "print(\"Needs processing:\", unprocessed)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "act2-3",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Step 3: Add order 1009 to the processed set, then\n",
    "# try to add 1001 (already there) — what happens?\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "act2-4",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Step 4: Remove order 1002 safely (use discard so it won't raise an error\n",
    "# even if the ID doesn't exist). Then try removing 9999.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "act2-answers",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "<details>\n",
    "<summary>💡 Show answers</summary>\n",
    "\n",
    "```python\n",
    "# Step 1\n",
    "unique_tags = set(raw_tags)\n",
    "# {'python', 'data', 'ml', 'ai'}\n",
    "\n",
    "# Step 2\n",
    "unprocessed = new_batch - processed\n",
    "# {1006, 1007, 1008}\n",
    "\n",
    "# Step 3\n",
    "processed.add(1009)  # added\n",
    "processed.add(1001)  # silent no-op — sets ignore duplicate adds\n",
    "\n",
    "# Step 4\n",
    "processed.discard(1002)  # removed cleanly\n",
    "processed.discard(9999)  # no error — discard is always safe\n",
    "# (compare: processed.remove(9999) would raise KeyError)\n",
    "```\n",
    "</details>\n",
    "\n",
    "---"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "act3-header",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Activity 3 — Gotcha Gauntlet\n",
    "Predict the output of each snippet **before** running it. Then discuss why."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "act3-a",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Snippet A — what type is this, and what does it contain?\n",
    "# Prediction: ___\n",
    "\n",
    "x = {}\n",
    "print(type(x))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "act3-b",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Snippet B — sets are unordered. What can we say about this output?\n",
    "# Prediction: ___\n",
    "\n",
    "s = {5, 3, 1, 4, 2}\n",
    "print(s)\n",
    "print(s[0])   # will this work?"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "act3-c",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Snippet C — what is the length? Why?\n",
    "# Prediction: ___\n",
    "\n",
    "data = set([True, 1, False, 0, 1, True])\n",
    "print(len(data))\n",
    "print(data)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "act3-d",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Snippet D — does order of difference matter?\n",
    "# Prediction: ___\n",
    "\n",
    "a = {1, 2, 3, 4}\n",
    "b = {3, 4, 5, 6}\n",
    "\n",
    "print(a - b)\n",
    "print(b - a)\n",
    "print(a - b == b - a)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "act3-e",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Snippet E — can you put a list inside a set? What about a tuple?\n",
    "# Prediction: ___\n",
    "\n",
    "try:\n",
    "    s = {[1, 2], [3, 4]}\n",
    "    print(\"list worked:\", s)\n",
    "except TypeError as e:\n",
    "    print(\"list failed:\", e)\n",
    "\n",
    "try:\n",
    "    s = {(1, 2), (3, 4)}\n",
    "    print(\"tuple worked:\", s)\n",
    "except TypeError as e:\n",
    "    print(\"tuple failed:\", e)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "act3-answers",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "<details>\n",
    "<summary>💡 Show answers + explanations</summary>\n",
    "\n",
    "```\n",
    "A → <class 'dict'>  — {} is an empty dict, not a set. Use set() for an empty set.\n",
    "\n",
    "B → The print output order is unpredictable — sets have no guaranteed order.\n",
    "    s[0] raises TypeError: 'set' object is not subscriptable. No indexing.\n",
    "\n",
    "C → len is 2, data is {False, True}  (or {0, 1})\n",
    "    In Python, True == 1 and False == 0, so they're treated as duplicates.\n",
    "    The set keeps whichever it saw first.\n",
    "\n",
    "D → a - b: {1, 2}    (in a but not b)\n",
    "    b - a: {5, 6}    (in b but not a)\n",
    "    a - b == b - a → False. Difference is NOT commutative (unlike union & intersection).\n",
    "\n",
    "E → list fails with: unhashable type: 'list'\n",
    "    Set elements must be hashable. Lists are mutable → not hashable.\n",
    "    Tuples are immutable → hashable → work fine as set elements.\n",
    "```\n",
    "</details>\n",
    "\n",
    "---"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "quiz-header",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Quiz — 6 Questions\n",
    "Write your prediction as a comment, then run to check."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "q1",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Q1: What does this print?\n",
    "# Prediction: ___\n",
    "\n",
    "a = {1, 2, 3}\n",
    "b = {3, 4, 5}\n",
    "print(a | b)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "q1-answer",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "<details><summary>Answer</summary>\n",
    "\n",
    "`{1, 2, 3, 4, 5}` — union includes all elements from both sets. No duplicates, so 3 appears only once.\n",
    "</details>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "q2",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Q2: What is the result, and what operation does it represent?\n",
    "# Prediction: ___\n",
    "\n",
    "a = {1, 2, 3, 4}\n",
    "b = {3, 4, 5, 6}\n",
    "print(a ^ b)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "q2-answer",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "<details><summary>Answer</summary>\n",
    "\n",
    "`{1, 2, 5, 6}` — symmetric difference: elements in either set but **not both**. 3 and 4 appear in both, so they're excluded.\n",
    "</details>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "q3",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Q3: Will this raise an error? If so, which line and why?\n",
    "# Prediction: ___\n",
    "\n",
    "s = {1, 2, 3}\n",
    "s.remove(3)\n",
    "s.remove(99)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "q3-answer",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "<details><summary>Answer</summary>\n",
    "\n",
    "Yes — `s.remove(99)` raises a `KeyError` because 99 is not in the set. Use `s.discard(99)` if you want a safe no-op instead.\n",
    "</details>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "q4",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Q4: True or False — sets preserve insertion order.\n",
    "# Test it and explain.\n",
    "\n",
    "s = set()\n",
    "for item in [\"banana\", \"apple\", \"cherry\", \"apple\", \"banana\"]:\n",
    "    s.add(item)\n",
    "print(s)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "q4-answer",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "<details><summary>Answer</summary>\n",
    "\n",
    "**False.** Sets are unordered — the output order is not guaranteed and may differ between runs or Python versions. Duplicates are silently dropped. If you need unique items in insertion order, use `dict.fromkeys([...])` instead.\n",
    "</details>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "q5",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Q5: What does this print, and what is the difference between the two approaches?\n",
    "# Prediction: ___\n",
    "\n",
    "a = {1, 2, 3}\n",
    "b = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5}\n",
    "\n",
    "print(a.issubset(b))\n",
    "print(a <= b)\n",
    "print(a < b)    # what does the strict version check?"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "q5-answer",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "<details><summary>Answer</summary>\n",
    "\n",
    "```\n",
    "True   # a is a subset of b\n",
    "True   # <= is equivalent to issubset()\n",
    "True   # < is a STRICT subset — a <= b AND a != b\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "`a < b` returns `False` if `a == b` (i.e. they contain exactly the same elements). It's the set equivalent of \"proper subset\".\n",
    "</details>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "q6",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Q6: Practical challenge — given two lists of user IDs,\n",
    "# find users who signed up in BOTH months using a one-liner.\n",
    "# Prediction: ___\n",
    "\n",
    "january = [101, 102, 103, 104, 105, 102, 103]\n",
    "february = [103, 104, 106, 107, 101]\n",
    "\n",
    "returning_users = ___\n",
    "print(returning_users)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "q6-answer",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "<details><summary>Answer</summary>\n",
    "\n",
    "```python\n",
    "returning_users = set(january) & set(february)\n",
    "# {101, 103, 104}\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "Converting to sets first drops duplicates within each month, then `&` finds the intersection. A clean, readable one-liner that would take several more lines with plain lists.\n",
    "</details>"
   ]
  }
 ],
